{
  "passage": "Meena watched her grandfather cut vegetables in the kitchen. He sharpened his knife on a stone before he began. 'Why do you do that?' she asked. 'A sharp knife cuts easily,' he said. Meena later learnt the reason in her science class. Pressure is the force acting on a unit area of a surface. When the same force acts on a smaller area, the pressure is greater. The sharp edge of a knife has a very small area, so the force from Grandfather's hand produces a large pressure and the knife cuts through a tomato easily. A blunt knife has a wider edge, so the same force spreads over a larger area and the pressure is less. The same idea explains many things Meena had seen. Porters carrying heavy loads on their heads place a round piece of cloth under the load. The cloth increases the area of contact, so the pressure on the head is reduced and the load is easier to carry. School bags have broad straps for the same reason, while thin straps cut painfully into the shoulders. Nails and pins have pointed tips so that they enter wood with little effort. Liquids and gases also exert pressure. Water presses on the walls of its container, and the pressure increases with depth. That is why the walls of a dam are made much thicker at the bottom than at the top.",
